{"id": "6f68ad03-d7b3-42db-ae59-5afc7ae6112c", "timestamp": "2026-10-19T23:02:26.363788", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "fa16c285-6baa-40c2-a580-8e2ccb6220f5", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "The researcher approved. I need to produce the full design document. I should ground claims about TorchOpt/torchviz in documentation/source. Network is disabled, so I can't fetch. I have knowledge of these libraries; I should cite docs from memory but flag that verification wasn't possible offline. Let me write the design document carefully.\n\nKey design points to make:\n\n**Operational definitions:**\n- Fused (assumption): TorchOpt-style = functional optimizer built on torch.func (functorch), where the update rule is expressed as a vmap-able pure function over the parameter pytree, using foreach-style batched tensor ops (torch._foreach_*) rather than per-parameter Python loops. Flagged as assumption.\n- Graph complexity: quantitative metrics on autograd graph of loss (nodes/edges/depth) and on re-materialized update-step graph (intermediate op count, dispatch count, fx node count).\n- Interpretability: label legibility ratio, diameter/branching, blinded-reader rubric.\n\n**Load-bearing methodological choice:** Re-materialize the update step with grad-enabled ops with state as differentiable leaves (torchviz-renderable), versus torch.fx trace / __torch_dispatch__ trace. Pick the grad-enabled re-run as primary because it feeds torchviz directly and produces the same representation as Arm A, enabling apples-to-apples make_dot comparison; use torch.fx/`__torch_dispatch__` (TorchDispatchMode) as a secondary objective count of aten ops executed by the real (no_grad) step, which anchors the re-materialized graph against reality. Limitations of re-materialization: (a) the real step runs under no_grad with in-place state updates; re-materializing with requires_grad state creates autograd nodes that don't exist in the real run and may change op selection (e.g., in-place ops like lerp_ become out-of-place); (b) foreach op decomposition in autograd graph differs from kernel-level count; (c) version-dependent internals of torch.optim.Adam (foreach default, capturable). Mitigations: also record TorchDispatchMode counts of the true step; report both.\n\nAlso negative control: deliberately non-fused functional implementation (per-tensor loop Adam in functional form, no foreach) — isolates \"functional\" from \"fused\": three arms actually: A) torch.optim.Adam default (foreach=True by default in recent torch actually), B) torchopt.Adam (torch.func-based), C) hand-written per-tensor functional Adam (no foreach ops). Maybe also A' with foreach=False. That gives a 2x2-ish factorial: functional vs stateful, fused(foreach) vs per-tensor.\n\nVersions to pin: torch 2.2.x? torchopt versions: torchopt 0.7.x requires torch>=1.13. Choose torch 2.3.1 + torchopt 0.7.3 (last release?) Actually torchopt latest ~0.7.x (0.7.3, May 2024). torchviz 0.0.2. graphviz python 0.20. I'll state: torch 2.3.1, torchopt 0.7.3, torchviz 0.0.2, pydot/graphviz. Reasons: torchopt 0.7.x supports torch.func API and pins torch>=1.13; torch 2.3 has stable torch.func and _foreach ops with decompositions. Note: exact versions are design choices to be confirmed at implementation time given the offline environment.\n\nMetrics extraction: parse dot via pydot; count nodes/edges; depth via BFS longest path; fx.graph node counts; TorchDispatchMode counter for aten ops per step.\n\nStatistical plan: descriptive primary (means ± CI across seeds × model sizes), maybe paired comparisons across sizes; interpretability rubric: N readers (e.g., 5), items scored 0–3, aggregate median/IQR, inter-rater agreement (Krippendorff's alpha or Cohen's κ), blinded to arm.\n\nScaling: model sizes e.g., width {32, 128, 512} MLP; also number of tensors varied by splitting parameters into groups.\n\nNumerical equivalence: same seed, compare updated params allclose within tolerance (foresee fp differences from foreach vs per-tensor; use float64 and tolerance 1e-7... Adam in float64 fine on CPU).\n\nNegative control: per-tensor functional Adam; prediction: it should look like/equal to unfused complexity; if fused arm doesn't reduce counts vs this control, hypothesis fails.\n\nAlso control for torch.optim.Adam default foreach: set foreach explicitly both True and False as sub-arms.\n\nFeasibility: pip install torch==2.3.1 torchopt==0.7.3 torchviz pydot in a Python 3.11–3.12 venv; CPU-only fine; graphviz system package for `dot` rendering (or use pydot to parse without rendering — actually can compute metrics from dot source; rendering SVG needs dot binary; could use pip's graphviz only for source generation and skip raster rendering, or install apt graphviz).\n\nCitations: I should cite TorchOpt docs (meta-optim paper arXiv 2210.00291? TorchOpt paper \"TorchOpt: An Efficient Library for PyTorch Optimization\" — JAX-inspired functional API, differentiable optimizers, based on functorch), torchviz docs (make_dot renders autograd graph via graphviz). I must be honest: offline, citations from memory, flagged.\n\nStructure the answer with sections 1–7 as requested. Keep it complete but reasonably concise. Evidence field: summarize repo observations.\n\nLet me draft the document.", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experiment Design: Gradient-Graph Complexity of Fused (TorchOpt-style) vs. TorchViz-Rendered Unfused Adam\n\n**Status: design only.** Nothing has been executed; no results are claimed. All library-internals claims below are cited from memory of public docs/source and are flagged for verification at implementation time (this sandbox has no network access to re-check them).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Operational definitions\n\n**1.1 \"Fused\" (ASSUMPTION, not established fact).** For this design, \"fused\" means: the Adam update is expressed as a *pure, vmap-able function over the parameter pytree* (torch.func/functorch functional API), whose per-tensor elementwise ops are *batched via foreach-style ops* (`torch._foreach_*`) or vectorized via `vmap`, instead of a Python loop issuing per-tensor ops. This mirrors TorchOpt's documented design (TorchOpt paper, arXiv:2210.00291; TorchOpt docs describe a JAX-style functional API built on functorch). *Caveat:* \"fused\" could alternatively mean kernel-level CUDA fusion only; under that reading the autograd-graph node count may not change at all. The design therefore measures both graph structure and executed-op counts to disambiguate.\n\n**1.2 \"Graph complexity\" (quantitative).** Measured on two objects per arm:\n- **G_loss:** the autograd graph of the scalar loss rendered by `torchviz.make_dot(loss, params)` (TorchViz renders the autograd DAG reachable from the loss to the named parameters — per torchviz docs).\n- **G_update:** the re-materialized optimizer-step graph (see §2) plus an executed-op trace of the *real* step (see §3).\n\nMetrics (computed programmatically, not eyeballed):\n- C1: node count (autograd `Function` nodes; fx nodes; aten op calls).\n- C2: edge count.\n- C3: graph depth (longest path from output to leaves).\n- C4: number of intermediate tensors created by the optimizer step.\n- C5: number of distinct aten ops dispatched per step (from TorchDispatchMode), distinguishing foreach vs per-tensor op calls.\n- C6: branching factor / diameter.\n\n**1.3 \"Interpretability\" (semi-quantitative).**\n- I1: label-legibility ratio — fraction of rendered nodes whose label names a recognizable mathematical operation vs. generic clutter (`AccumulateGrad`, `ViewBackward`, anonymous aten ops).\n- I2: minimum number of nodes a reader traverses to trace one parameter's update from gradient to new value.\n- I3: blinded-reader rubric — 5 independent raters, blinded to arm, score each rendered graph (anonymized, order-randomized) on a 0–3 scale for four items: (a) can identify the gradient-descent direction term, (b) can identify the first-moment (m) update, (c) can identify the second-moment (v) update with bias correction, (d) overall readability. Aggregated per §5.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The load-bearing methodological choice: exposing optimizer-internal ops\n\n**Problem.** `torch.optim.Adam.step()` and TorchOpt's in-place apply run under `torch.no_grad()` (optimizer internals create no autograd nodes; TorchViz cannot see them). TorchViz on the loss alone shows only the forward/backward graph, which is *identical* across arms and thus uninformative about the optimizer.\n\n**Primary method (chosen): grad-enabled re-materialization of the update step.** For each arm, write a *graph-visible twin* of the update: the same arithmetic (bias-corrected m, v, ratio, parameter delta) re-executed with (i) gradients treated as ordinary differentiable leaves (`g = grad.clone().detach().requires_grad_(True)`), (ii) optimizer state (m, v) as differentiable leaves, (iii) all ops out-of-place, (iv) the resulting pseudo-update `Δ` retained as a graph output. Render `make_dot(Δ.sum(), {g, m, v, ...})`. Rationale for choosing this over tracing: it produces a representation in the *same formalism and renderer* as Arm A's loss graph, so the comparison is apples-to-apples in node semantics, not just in counts; and every node corresponds to an autograd Function that a TorchViz reader would actually see.\n\n**Limitations of the primary method (stated explicitly):**\n- L1: The real step runs under `no_grad` with in-place state mutation; the twin's out-of-place, grad-enabled execution may select different op variants (e.g., `lerp_` → `lerp`, fused `addcmul_` variants decompose differently) and always adds autograd bookkeeping nodes. The twin therefore *overstates* node counts and is comparable only *between arms*, not to reality.\n- L2: Foreach ops may decompose into many aten ops in the autograd graph even though they dispatch as few kernels — the twin may *understate* the fusion benefit at graph level while C5 (dispatch counts) shows it. Both views are reported and discrepancies are discussed, not averaged away.\n- L3: Twin correctness must be validated (§4 numerical equivalence) so it performs the same math as the real step.\n\n**Secondary method (anchoring): `torch.__torch_dispatch__` (TorchDispatchMode) counter** wrapped around the *real* optimizer step in each arm, logging every aten op actually dispatched (op name, tensor count). This is objective, needs no graph semantics, and validates/corrects L1–L2. Additionally, `torch.fx.symbolic_trace` of the functional update function gives a third, structure-only count (C1/C3) for the fused arm's update function.\n\n**Defense:** no single representation answers \"is the fused graph less complex *as rendered by TorchViz*?\" The twin answers the rendered-graph question directly; dispatch counting answers the \"did fusion actually reduce operations?\" question; the two together let us state whether any interpretability gain is real or a rendering artifact.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Fixed comparison setup\n\n- **Model:** MLP, 3 hidden layers, widths {32, 128, 512} (scaling axis). Fixed init seed. `dtype=float64`, device CPU (avoids foreach-vs-CUDA-only fused kernels; float64 for tight numerical equivalence).\n- **Loss:** fixed regression loss (MSE) on one fixed synthetic batch (seeded).\n- **Library versions (pinned; to be confirmed installable at implementation time):** `torch==2.3.1` (stable `torch.func`, mature `_foreach_*` ops, both Adam foreach variants), `torchopt==0.7.3` (functional API + `extract_state_dict`; pins torch>=1.13 per its docs), `torchviz==0.0.2`, `pydot>=2.0`, `networkx>=3`. Python 3.11 or 3.12 venv (torchopt/torch wheels do not yet target 3.14 — consistent with the bare sandbox).\n- **Only the optimizer path varies between arms;** model, loss, batch, seed, dtype, and rendering settings (`make_dot` params, `show_attrs`) are identical.\n- **Arm A (stateful, unfused reference):** `torch.optim.Adam(..., foreach=False)`, hand-matched hyperparameters (lr=1e-3, betas=(0.9,0.999), eps=1e-8).\n- **Arm A′ (stateful, foreach):** `torch.optim.Adam(..., foreach=True)` — isolates the foreach contribution within PyTorch itself.\n- **Arm B (TorchOpt-style, fused):** `torchopt.Adam` functional API (`torchopt.extract_state_dict` / functional update over the pytree).\n- **Arm C (negative control):** hand-written *per-tensor-loop* functional Adam (pure function, no foreach, no vmap) — functional but deliberately unfused. Prediction: C ≈ A in complexity. If B does not beat C, any measured \"fusion\" gain is not attributable to TorchOpt's fusion.\n- Arms are run for one step (graph structure is step-invariant after step 1) and also at step k to check for state-dependent structural changes.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Controls and validity checks\n\n- **V1 Numerical equivalence:** after one step from identical init, updated parameters across A, A′, B, C must satisfy `torch.allclose(..., rtol=1e-7, atol=1e-9)` (float64). If B differs beyond tolerance, its graph comparison is invalid (it is not doing the same math) and the discrepancy is reported before any complexity claim.\n- **V2 Matched tensor structure:** all arms operate on the same parameter pytree (same number of tensors, same shapes) — prevents foreach batching from changing the *grouping* rather than the ops.\n- **V3 Twin fidelity:** for each arm, the multiset of aten op names in the twin graph must be a superset-consistent, explainable transformation of the dispatch-mode trace of the real step; unexplained ops invalidate the twin for that arm.\n- **V4 Scaling:** metrics reported per width {32, 128, 512}; fusion effects that only appear at many-tensor scale will show as an arm×width interaction, not a constant offset.\n- **V5 Negative control:** Arm C (see §3) plus a \"trivial control\" — SGD (no state): its update graph is ~3 nodes, establishing floor values for all metrics.\n- **V6 Rendering parity:** identical `make_dot` arguments across arms; rendered `.dot` sources archived so counts are re-derivable.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Analysis plan\n\n- **Extraction:** parse `.dot` output with `pydot` → node list, edge list; depth/diameter via networkx longest-path/BFS; dispatch counts from a TorchDispatchMode log (JSON); fx node counts from `torch.fx` graphs. All counts emitted to CSV (one row per arm × width × seed × metric).\n- **Summaries:** primarily descriptive — per-metric medians with IQR across ≥10 seeds per cell; paired per-seed differences (B−A, B−C) with bootstrap 95% CIs (no distributional claims needed for a design answer); a scaling table (metric vs width) to detect interaction.\n- **Interpretability aggregation:** per-item rater scores → per-graph mean; arm-level medians and IQRs; inter-rater reliability via Krippendorff's α (ordinal); if α < 0.5, interpretability results are reported as inconclusive rather than narrated. Raters see anonymized graphs in randomized order; one rater (the designer) is excluded from the blinded set.\n- **Pre-registered expectation (stated as prediction, not result):** Arm B shows fewer per-step dispatch ops (C5) and smaller G_update than A/C; G_loss is identical across arms by construction — if not, the setup is broken.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Feasibility (honest accounting for the bare environment)\n\n- Current sandbox: Python 3.14.4, **no** torch/torchviz/torchopt/graphviz installed, **no network**, no GPU. No part of this design can execute here, and none needs to.\n- A real execution requires: a Python 3.11/3.12 venv; `pip install torch==2.3.1 torchopt==0.7.3 torchviz pydot networkx`; a system GraphViz install (`dot`) only if image rendering (not just `.dot` parsing) is wanted — metrics extraction needs only the dot source; CPU-only is sufficient (models are tiny; foreach works on CPU).\n- Effort estimate: twin implementations (~100 lines per arm), dispatch-mode wrapper (~30 lines), extraction/rubric scripts — a day-scale implementation task, not a compute task.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Explicit separation of what is fixed vs. chosen vs. assumed\n\n**(a) Fixed by the task/brief:** design-only; the research question as stated in task.json; comparison of fused (TorchOpt-style) vs. TorchViz-rendered Adam graphs; the requirement that the researcher may clarify setup details; no execution, no fabricated results.\n\n**(b) Design choices and rationale (mine):** grad-enabled re-materialization as the primary graph-exposure method (§2, defended); TorchDispatchMode trace + fx as anchors (chosen for objectivity, addresses twin limitations L1–L2); the 4-arm factorial (A, A′, B, C + SGD floor) to separate \"functional\" from \"fused\" and to provide a negative control; float64/CPU for equivalence checking; pydot/networkx programmatic extraction; blinded-rubric aggregation with a reliability gate; width scaling {32,128,512}; pinned versions torch 2.3.1 / torchopt 0.7.3 / torchviz 0.0.2 chosen for stable `torch.func` + `_foreach_*` support and torchopt compatibility — exact pins are implementation-time confirmations.\n\n**(c) Assumptions and uncertainties (flagged, with sourcing status):**\n- A1 (load-bearing assumption): TorchOpt's \"fusion\" = functorch-functional + foreach/vmap-batched update ops. Source: TorchOpt docs/paper (arXiv:2210.00291) describe the functional, functorch-based design; the precise fused-op set per torchopt 0.7.3 must be verified against its source (`torchopt/_src/adam.py`) at implementation time.\n- A2: torchviz renders only autograd graphs of a scalar output w.r.t. given parameters (torchviz README); it cannot see `no_grad` optimizer internals — hence §2's workaround.\n- U1: If fusion is purely kernel-level, graph-node counts may not differ across arms; the design detects this but the *hypothesis itself* may fail — that is a finding, not a design flaw.\n- U2: The twin's op set may diverge from the real step across torch versions (in-place/foreach decomposition changes); mitigated by V3 but not eliminable.\n- U3: Interpretability measurement is inherently sub